# Copy to .env beside docker-compose.yml. This file is the whole of a deployment's
# configuration: the compose file holds nothing worth changing, so nothing there needs
# editing to install this somewhere else.
#
# ── Fill these eight, and nothing else has to be touched ─────────────────────────
#
#     SERVER_IMAGE     the version to run
#     POSTGRES_PASSWORD, SERVER_SECRET   generated, and kept
#     PUBLISH_PORT     the host port
#     RUN_AS           the uid:gid that owns the directories below
#     DB_DIR, BLOB_DIR, BACKUP_DIR, STATE_DIR    four directories YOU create first
#
# The four directories have no defaults on purpose: compose refuses to start without them,
# rather than quietly writing an installation's whole state into ./data beside this file,
# where the next person to tidy up would find something that looks like scratch.

# ── Secrets. Both required, neither has a default ────────────────────────────────
# A compose file that supplied one would be a secret in git; an unset one would be a
# database open to whatever else runs on the host. Generate each with:
#
#     openssl rand -base64 32
#
POSTGRES_PASSWORD=
# Signs access tokens, the delta cursor and the salt an unknown login receives. Losing it
# invalidates every session and every cursor; leaking it forges all three. It is NOT in
# the database, so a database backup does not carry it — keep it wherever the rest of this
# host's secrets live.
SERVER_SECRET=

# ── Where the data lives on the host ─────────────────────────────────────────────
# **Create these four before starting**, and put them somewhere a new build cannot reach:
# installing one replaces the checkout, so anything inside it is not storage. They are the
# whole of the server's state — lose them and the installation is gone.
#
# Three must be writable by RUN_AS (`chmod 775` is usually enough, and `chown` usually needs
# a root you do not have). DB_DIR is the exception and is not yours to arrange: the postgres
# image takes that directory over as its own user, so create it and leave it alone.
#
#     mkdir -p /srv/syncserver/{db,blobs,backups,state}
#     chmod 775 /srv/syncserver/{blobs,backups,state}
#
DB_DIR=
BLOB_DIR=
# Where a backup copy lands on this host. BACKUP_DESTINATION below is the container's side of
# this same mount, and naming one without the other writes the copy into the container's
# writable layer, where the next `docker compose pull` takes it.
BACKUP_DIR=
# The restore epoch: the one file that must survive restoring the database AND the blobs,
# which is why it is neither of them. It is how a restored database is noticed at all.
STATE_DIR=

# ── Identity and port ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# The uid:gid the server runs as. BLOB_DIR must be writable by it, or the server starts and
# then fails on the first upload rather than at boot — the later failure being the confusing
# one. DB_DIR is not yours to arrange: the postgres image takes that directory over as its
# own user, so create it and leave it alone.
#
# `deploy-dev-host.sh` OVERWRITES this with the uid:gid of whoever runs it, because that is
# the user who creates the data directories. The value below is a plausible default for a
# hand-rolled install and is wrong on most NAS boxes, where the administrator is not 1000.
RUN_AS=1000:1000
# The host port. Inside the container it is always 8080, so change this rather than
# anything in the compose file.
#
# On a NAS, 8080 is usually taken by the management interface, and the clash surfaces late:
# the database comes up healthy, the image builds, and only then does the daemon refuse the
# port. Pick something else there.
PUBLISH_PORT=8080

# ── Rarely changed ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# POSTGRES_DB=syncserver
# POSTGRES_USER=syncserver
#
# The server image, pinned to a version. `latest` is deliberately not used: a server
# updated a few times a year must be able to say what it is running, and to go back. The
# image is published from CI (docs/13); the default tracks the release in package.json.
# SERVER_IMAGE=ghcr.io/olegg90/syncserver:<version>   # the releases page has the current one
#
# ── Backups ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# All three must be set together, or the console's backup button answers "not
# configured". The dump command names `pg_dump` (or a wrapper) and any arguments it needs;
# the server appends `-f <destination>/<run>/database.dump`.
#
# `pg_dump` is IN the image, at the same major the database runs, so this line needs no path
# and no install step — it is left uncommented for that reason. The other two are still the
# operator's to choose: a destination is a mount only this installation knows about, and
# leaving it unset is what keeps the button honestly answering "not configured".
#
# BACKUP_DESTINATION is the path INSIDE the container, and /backups is where compose mounts
# BACKUP_DIR from this machine — the same split as BLOB_DIR and /data/blobs. Naming a path
# with no mount behind it writes the copy into the container's own layer, where the next
# `docker compose pull` takes it: a backup-shaped delay rather than a backup.
# BACKUP_DESTINATION=/backups
BACKUP_DB_COMMAND="pg_dump --format=custom"
# And how to put one BACK (#155), used by `restore-cli.js` — a different binary with different flags, so
# it is its own variable rather than derived from the one above. `--clean --if-exists` is what makes it
# replace what is there instead of colliding with it. The dump file is appended by the command itself.
# RESTORE_DB_COMMAND="pg_restore --clean --if-exists --no-owner -d"
#
# How often to LOAD the newest backup into a scratch database, rather than only checking that its blobs
# are present (#159). Weekly by default: it restores a whole dump, which is minutes rather than the second
# the blob check takes. `0` turns it off for a deployment that rehearses by hand — the honest opt-out,
# since the point of a schedule is that nobody remembers to.
#
# It needs the database role to be able to CREATE DATABASE. Where it cannot, the server says so and
# carries on: a check that stopped the thing it checks would be the worst trade in the system.
# REHEARSE_RESTORE_EVERY_SECONDS=604800
# BACKUP_BLOB_SOURCE=/data/blobs
#
# How often a backup is TAKEN, once the three above are set. Defaulted to a day, and ON: the
# opt-in already happened when a destination was chosen, and an unattended server with backups
# configured and none being taken is the failure this exists to prevent. Each run verifies the
# copy it just wrote and says so in the log — which is the only surface an unattended box has.
#
# Set to 0 if backups are driven from outside (cron on the host, a NAS snapshot job): two
# schedules mean two refusal windows where the operator asked for one.
# BACKUP_EVERY_SECONDS=86400
#
# How many finished copies to KEEP on disk. Unset means all of them, which is what every
# deployment has done until now — deleting backups is not a behaviour to acquire by upgrading.
#
# Set it and each scheduled run prunes what falls past the newest N, AFTER taking its own copy:
# pruning first would delete an old backup to make room for one that then failed. The newest
# good copy is never removed, whatever this says. Every removal is logged, because a server
# that silently deletes backups is indistinguishable from one that loses them.
# BACKUP_KEEP=7
#
# Where the server records the newest restore_epoch it has run with. It must survive a
# restore, so it lives OUTSIDE the database dump and the blob store — a file path on the
# host, not inside either.
# RESTORE_STATE_FILE names the path INSIDE the container; /state is where compose mounts
# STATE_DIR from this machine. It must be on a mount: the file's whole job is to outlive the
# database it describes and the container that wrote it, so one in the container's own layer
# is a guard that forgets exactly when it is needed.
# RESTORE_STATE_FILE=/state/restore.epoch
